a frame that half-wrote leaves no record of itself

This is leticl's finding, put to me as a question — *"ask what YOUR paint does after a
partial failure"* — and the answer is **the same defect, in the same shape**:

    paint_full(shown: &mut Vec<String>, …) -> String   // and inside it:
        shown[i] = l.clone();                          // ← recorded while BUILDING

    let _ = out.write_all(s.as_bytes());                // ← error discarded

The memory of the glass was updated *while the bytes were being built*, and the write
that followed threw its error away. So a frame that died part-way left this head
believing rows were on the glass that it had never written. The next frame skipped
them, on the entirely legitimate rule that a row whose text has not changed need not be
sent (`if shown[i] == *l { continue; }`), so **the hole was permanent** — and the only
things that could clear it were the two that force a full repaint: Ctrl-L, and **a
resize**.

## That last word is the operator's own report, and it is why this is worth a commit

> *"when i expand tools with Ct scroll stops working, even after collapsing back. i
> have to switch byobu windows back and forth"*

The explanation that went into the log for that was **escapes in a tool payload**
turning off mouse reporting — real, and fixed by `without_control`, with a test. But a
window switch *resizes*, and a resize is one of the two things that clears a bad glass
memory. `scroll_still_works_after_ctrl_t` even writes the symptom down in its own doc —
*"a window switch is a resize, which forces the frame this was waiting for"* — and then
does not follow it. **So this is the other half of that report, and it is the half that
explained the repair.**

## The fix: separate what a frame *intends* from what the glass *is*

`paint`/`paint_full` no longer mutate their input. They return **the bytes and the
glass-state those bytes would leave behind**, and the caller adopts it — which is the
only place that knows whether it went out:

- **A write that succeeded** adopts the new memory. One statement later than it was, and
  that statement is the whole fix.
- **A write that failed** clears the memory and sets `full`, so the next frame is a
  whole-screen repaint.
- **A frame that needed no bytes** adopts the new memory without writing, because a
  frame that needed no bytes is one whose memory is already true (and taking it keeps
  `shown` the same length as the frame, which matters for a frame that got taller).

**Why "forget" and not "work out how much landed".** A `write_all` that fails may have
written any prefix, so the head cannot know which rows are up. It could parse its own
output for cursor moves and count what fitted — and a mistake there puts the hole back,
silently, which is the entire class of defect being fixed here. One extra frame after a
failure, and no way to be wrong instead.

**And the counters follow the same rule.** Bytes, row rewrites and clears are counted
only on the write that carried them: a frame that failed is a frame *attempted*, and
`WriteStats::bytes` claiming bytes that never reached the glass is the same defect as
`shown` claiming rows that never reached it.

## The seam, because the interesting half needs no pty

`draw_with_cursor` is now `paint_to(&mut stdout(), …)`, and `paint_to` takes the writer.
That is what makes the failure testable at all: `Terminal::enter` needs a real terminal
and nothing about the diff, the cursor or the failure policy does. The test uses a
writer that **fails on its second call** on purpose — by then the frame's opening bytes
really are on the glass, so "commit nothing" and "commit everything" are both wrong and
the assertion has to be about *what the head knows* rather than what it sent.

## A test that cannot fail guards nothing — this tree's instance of it

The operator's second point, and this tree has an instance, so it is named rather than
left for the next reader:

`scroll_still_works_after_ctrl_t` **cannot catch the defect it is named for.** It asserts
`a.scroll` and `a.payload_page` — *head* state — and the defect was in `shown`, *glass*
state. The head's scroll counter was moving perfectly the whole time; the screen was
not repainting. It would pass with the bug present, which is what it did.

It is left in place, because it does guard the thing it can see: that the arrows belong
to the payload while a window is open and come back on Esc, and that `ctrl-t` no longer
moves the transcript. What it cannot see is the glass, and no test in `app.rs` can —
`app.rs` never composes bytes. **The guard for the glass is
`a_paint_that_dies_part_way_leaves_no_record`, in the file that owns the encoder**, and
its first assertion is that the premise holds (bytes really went out before the failure),
so it is not a test that arranges its signal the way the bug did not.

## Tests

- `a_paint_that_dies_part_way_leaves_no_record` — a good frame, then one whose write dies
  after the opener: the head claims **nothing** (not the old frame, not the new one), the
  next frame is a `full` repaint carrying every row, and the byte counters are zero.
- Five existing paint tests were rewritten for the new shape, and **two of them got
  stronger**: `an_unchanged_frame_writes_nothing_at_all` now also asserts the memory is
  adopted every time (a `paint` that returned its input would answer `""` by never having
  learned anything), and the height test keeps the glass honest across a grow *and* a
  shrink.

`cargo test -p letibot-tui`: **307 passed, 0 failed, 4 ignored** (306 + 1).

**Not verified, and said rather than glossed:** `rustfmt --check` cannot run — it writes
a temp file and `bash` is closed for that direction by the circuit breaker, which only
the operator lifts. One cargo at a time from here on: a five-crate run started earlier
sat at 0 % CPU for twenty-six minutes because three of my own `cargo` invocations were
contending on `target/`, which is why every run named above is one crate.

## Not in this commit

**§3.1, and this defect is one route into it.** A raw escape in *model prose* still
reaches the glass verbatim — tool payloads are neutralised by `without_control`, and
nothing else is. That is the same class as this commit's: `shown` can diverge from the
glass without any write failing, because a `\r` or a mode set inside a text row moves
the cursor in ways the head's model of the row does not describe. leticl is a proof that
this matters (it composes into a cell grid and cannot emit an escape it did not author),
and it has two tests where this tree has none — that is the next item.
